  },
  "subtitle": "[ASSET: left | SlideRule | width=15%] As for this month's VMA, there really was no contest.\n[BR]\n[BR] The award <i>must</i> go to Iron Man's \"built-in slide rule calculater\" that made its appearance in Tony's armor in [LINK:1963:TOS_50_iron0|TOS #50|2027-03-24].\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Classic!]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(But note that the word \"calculator\" is actually misspelled.)]\n\n[ASSET: right | MoreRugged | width=12%] But we can't ignore the laugh-inducing panel in this month's [LINK:1963:AVEN_3|AVEN #3|2027-03-01], where page 8 shows Don Blake closing his office early (to respond to a summons from Iron Man), only to show Jane Foster's thoughts of:\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"Oh, Don, if only you didn't <i>pamper</i> yourself so!&nbsp; If only you were more <i>rugged</i>!\"]\n[BR] <b><i>Hahahaha!</i></b>  Good enough for an Honorable Mention!",
  "review_text": "This month featured nine graded stories, spread over eight issues (two again from <i>Strange Tales</i>), yielded a final weighted average of [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=large; fonttype=bold; text=5.74], or about a point and a quarter improvement over last month's <b>4.51</b>.\n\nThis marks the first time since July that the stories were <i>well</i> above just the reader level, and in fact, 4 of the 9 stories were actually at mid-grade or above (and a couple of near misses to boot).&nbsp; It's clear from the letters coming in that there is an overall sense of excitement growing in the readership over the general direction of the books.\n\nThe improved characterization of the X-Men was a noticeable plus this month, and the Hulk/Sub-Mariner \"super-villain team-up\" in <i>Avengers</i> was also decent.&nbsp; Doom's umpteenth appearance in <i>Fantastic Four</i> managed a mid-grade rating, as did the Iron Man story involving his first encounter with the Mandarin.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=But the real star of the month was <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i>, which showed that a new villain in the pages of a book (Electro) isn't always even the best thing about the story.]\n\nHowever, [SPAN: severity=error; text=on the negative side] was the positively <i>abysmal</i> performance by both halves of <i>Strange Tales</i>, with neither the Human Torch nor Doctor Strange doing anything to prevent me from wondering why I'm reading them at all!\n\n[DIVIDER]\nWhile I'm on the subject of Doctor Strange, I feel compelled to add the following proclamation:\n\nLongbox Playback archivists have recently uncovered evidence that Baron Mordo's earliest appearances were actually orchestrated by [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Baron Bored-o, Dread Lord of Narrative Drag.]\n\nScholars disagree on whether Bored-o acted alone or simply amplified Mordo's natural tendency toward plot-induced tedium.&nbsp; But it makes little difference, as the net effect of his presence almost <i>guarantees</i> the reader a one-way trip to the Land of Nod.\n\nNo one who has read my reviews on the issues featuring Mordo could question why I hereby christen him thusly.\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=So let it be said -- So let it be <b>done</b>!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThe three main backup features that are at least <i>marginally</i> related to Marvel's main continuity are:\n[HANG] * Tales of Asgard\n[HANG] * The Wonderful Wasp Tells a Tale\n[HANG] * Tales of the Watcher\n\nThey all differ in the way they're told, with <i>Asgard</i> being a straight-forward 3<sup>rd</sup> person tale, while the Wasp seemingly makes hers up just to entertain her orphan listeners.&nbsp; On the other hand, the Watcher uses his platform to turn to the reader at the end and essentially say, [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"And the moral of the story is...\"]\n\nA couple of the split books also have completely unrelated backup stories, but one can already see the handwriting on the wall:&nbsp; they are slowly morphing over to feature mainline characters from the Marvel Universe, even though a couple of them still are simply the \"astonishing\" or \"suspenseful\" tales readers would have gotten <i>anyway</i>, dressed up to look as if they have some connection to the main continuity.\n\n(And, by the way, I'm not saying this is a <i>bad</i> thing -- but it <i>is</i> happening, whether readers like it or not.)\n\n\n[DEVNOTE: *** SUMMARY_BEGIN ***\n*** THIS MONTH ***9 graded stories over 8 issues...TOTAL WEIGHT: 6.83 grade character issue    weight 9.2   asm       ASM #9   1.00 6.5   xmen      XMEN #3  1.00 6.0   ff        FF #23   1.00 6.0   iron      TOS #50  0.50 5.5   aven      AVEN #3  1.25 5.5   thor      JIM #100 0.50 4.5   giant     TTA #52  0.75 1.8   torch     ST #117  0.50 1.8   strange   ST #117  0.335.74*** LAST MONTH ***8 graded stories over 6 issues...TOTAL WEIGHT: 4.58 grade character issue   weight 6.5   asm       ASM #8  0.75 6.5   ff        FF #22  1.00 4.5   iron      TOS #49 0.75 4.0   thor      JIM #99 0.50 4.0   giant     TTA #51 0.50 2.0   asm       ASM #8  0.25 2.0   strange   ST #116 0.33 1.5   torch     ST #116 0.504.51\n*** SUMMARY_END ***]",
  "letters_page": "[HANG0] * Introduction of Electro, Mandarin, Blob, Black Knight (Nathan Garrett -- not that <i>other</i> Black Knight!), and (<i>shudder</i>) the Terrible Trio.\n[HANG0] * Sub-Mariner/Hulk \"super villain team-up\" in Avengers\n[HANG0] * Improved characterization of the X-Men, perhaps in response to readers who were saying they were no different than the FF, particularly the Beast.\n\n[DEVNOTE: *** INTRODUCTION_BEGIN ***\nBlack Knight (Nathan Garrett) | villain | Milestone: (first)\nBlob | villain | Milestone: (first)\nElectro | villain | Milestone: (first), (origin)\nNotes | first | Dr. Bromwell (Nicholas)\nNotes | first | villain | Terrible Trio (Bull Brogin, Yogi Dakor, Harry Phillips)\nMandarin | villain | Milestone: (first)\n*** INTRODUCTION_END ***]\n\n<b>Roundup:</b>\n[DEVNOTE: *** HIGHLIGHTS_BEGIN ***]\n[HANG0] * The Sub-Mariner joins the fun, forcing the Avengers to revisit their favorite pastime:&nbsp; fighting the Hulk.\n[HANG0] * Thor defeats neither Mr. Hyde nor fate when Jane's intervention draws Odin's wrath.\n[HANG0] * Giant-Man, the Wasp, and the newly minted Black Knight spend months getting to a story that barely moves the needle.\n[HANG0] * Professor X's open-door mutant policy backfires spectacularly when the Blob arrives, but the team itself takes an important step forward.\n[HANG0] * Electro brings the electricity, but Spider-Man's personal life provides the sparks.\n[HANG0] * Even a surplus of Doom can't overshadow the growing chemistry of the cast, though Reed has to pause to explain an Earth-shattering phenomenon nobody has apparently noticed before.\n[HANG0] * The Human Torch takes on the Eel's return engagement, while readers wonder who asked for one.\n[HANG0] * Dr. Strange vs. Baron Mordo -- a replica house, a cylinder trap, and nothing resembling a motive.\n[HANG0] * A new villain arrives in the form of the Mandarin, but the <i>real</i> star is Iron Man's calculator.\n[DEVNOTE: *** HIGHLIGHTS_END ***]",
  "ownership": "The end of the 1963 calendar year promises the following:\n[HANG0] * the Tomorrow Man is back to plague Thor\n[HANG0] * the Porcupine returns to <i>stick</i> it to Giant-Man and the Wasp\n[HANG0] * the Human Torch has to contend with the Wizard yet again\n[HANG0] * Doctor Strange meets a bunch of possessed people\n[HANG0] * A terrible infant is on the loose, and the FF have to rein him in somehow\n[HANG0] * Iron Man is menaced by a scarecrow\n[HANG0] * and who the heck is the Big Man and his Enforcers?&nbsp; Let's hope that Spider-Man doesn't have to learn the hard way!"
}